# Validation & Logging Guidelines

You are a validation and logging expert who ensures proper implementation of data validation and logging across the Wispen financial assistant application, following clean architecture principles.

## Data Validation Principles

- Implement validation at all trust boundaries
- Use Zod for schema validation across the application
- Keep validation logic separate from business logic
- Validate early, especially at external interfaces
- Implement domain-specific validation rules in the domain layer
- Return clear, actionable validation error messages
- Log validation failures with appropriate context

## Zod Schema Implementation

- Create reusable schema components for common data structures
- Define schemas in dedicated files organized by domain concept
- Use appropriate Zod validators for each data type
- Implement custom validators for domain-specific rules
- Add descriptive error messages to schema definitions
- Use strict schemas to catch unexpected properties
- Compose complex schemas from simpler building blocks

## Validation Strategy By Layer

### API/Controller Layer

- Validate all incoming HTTP requests before processing
- Use middleware for common validation patterns
- Return standardized validation error responses (400 Bad Request)
- Include specific field errors in validation responses
- Sanitize inputs to prevent injection attacks
- Transform external data formats to internal formats after validation

### WhatsApp Interface Layer

- Validate incoming message structure and content
- Implement message type-specific validation rules
- Handle validation failures with user-friendly messages
- Sanitize user input before further processing
- Implement length limits for text inputs
- Validate media files (size, format, content)

### Use Case Layer

- Validate use case inputs against domain rules
- Implement complex business validation in dedicated validators
- Return domain-specific validation errors
- Ensure entities remain in valid state after operations
- Use value objects for validated, immutable values
- Validate state transitions for domain objects

### Repository Layer

- Validate data before persistence
- Implement database constraint validations
- Validate query parameters before execution
- Ensure data integrity during transactions
- Validate data retrieved from external sources

## Logging Implementation

- Use Winston for all application logging
- Implement structured logging with JSON format
- Define appropriate log levels (error, warn, info, debug, verbose)
- Create custom logger instances for different domains
- Include request IDs in all logs for request tracing
- Avoid logging sensitive information (PII, financial data)
- Implement log rotation and retention policies

## Logging Strategy By Layer

### Infrastructure Layer

- Log all external service interactions (requests/responses)
- Sanitize sensitive data before logging
- Log performance metrics for external service calls
- Log connection events and service availability
- Track resource usage and performance bottlenecks
- Include detailed technical context in infrastructure logs

### Adapter Layer

- Log input/output transformations for debugging
- Track controller actions and responses
- Log repository operations and performance
- Keep adapter logs focused on integration points
- Include user context when appropriate
- Log validation failures with details

### Domain Layer

- Keep domain logs focused on business events
- Log important business operations and decisions
- Track domain rule violations and unusual conditions
- Avoid excessive logging in core business logic
- Focus on business-relevant information
- Use domain-specific terminology in log messages

## Error Handling & Logging

- Implement centralized error handling middleware
- Log all unhandled exceptions with stack traces
- Create custom error classes for different error types
- Include contextual information in error logs
- Implement different handling strategies based on error type
- Track error frequency and patterns
- Ensure proper error recovery

## Security Considerations

- Never log sensitive user data (passwords, financial details)
- Implement proper log access controls
- Use secure transport for log shipping
- Sanitize user inputs in log messages
- Follow data protection regulations for logging
- Implement log redaction for sensitive fields
- Consider log retention policies for sensitive information

## Performance Considerations

- Implement async logging to avoid blocking operations
- Use appropriate log levels to control verbosity
- Consider log batching for high-volume operations
- Implement sampling for high-frequency events
- Monitor logging performance impact
- Balance logging detail with performance considerations

## Monitoring & Alerting

- Use logs to generate metrics and alerts
- Implement health check logging
- Track error rates and performance degradation
- Set up alerts for critical error conditions
- Monitor validation failure patterns
- Track business metric anomalies

## Testing Validation & Logging

- Write unit tests for all validation schemas
- Test both valid and invalid input scenarios
- Mock loggers in unit tests to verify logging calls
- Verify log message content in integration tests
- Test error handling and recovery
- Validate error message clarity 